title: "BORDER OPTICS: Satellite Verification of India's Vibrant Villages Programme"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/BORDER_OPTICS"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21759970"
    description: "Zenodo archive of BORDER OPTICS v1.0.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21759970"
abstract: >-
  An independent satellite-verification study of India's Vibrant Villages
  Programme (VVP-I), a ₹4,800 crore border-area development scheme sanctioned
  across 2,967 villages in five Himalayan border states/union territories.
  Using Sentinel-2 built-up-index (NDBI) and VIIRS night-lights data at 258
  individually geocoded villages against a matched non-VVP control group of
  735 villages in the same 14 districts, this study tests whether VVP-I's
  priority villages show measurable physical development since programme
  sanction, under two independent compositing windows (full-year and
  summer-matched) as an explicit robustness check rather than trusting a
  single satellite comparison. A full-year composite shows no significant
  increase in built-up area, while a season-matched composite on the same
  villages shows the opposite result — a reversal traced to snow-cover
  contamination and monsoon cloud cover respectively. A district-fixed-effects
  Difference-in-Differences model against the control group reproduces this
  same window-sensitivity pattern for built-up area (significant summer gap,
  null full-year gap), survives a buffer-radius sweep (250m/500m/1km) and
  Holm-Bonferroni correction for multiple testing, and a three-point
  2021/2023/2025 extension shows the summer-matched result is concentrated in
  a 2023-to-2025 recovery rather than steady growth since sanction.
  Night-lights show no absolute before/after increase of their own in either
  window, but do show a significant positive gap against the control group in
  both windows — driven by control villages declining rather than treated
  villages rising. The instability itself is reported as the central finding,
  addressing a parliamentary record showing no independent impact assessment
  has ever been carried out for VVP-I.
keywords:
  - Vibrant Villages Programme
  - border development
  - securitization theory
  - satellite verification
  - NDBI
  - VIIRS night-lights
  - remote sensing
  - robustness testing
  - difference-in-differences
  - India
  - geospatial analysis
